    "0.21": {
        "info": {
            "author": "Homagni Saha",
            "author_email": "Homagni Saha <homagnisaha@gmail.com>",
            "bugtrack_url": null,
            "classifiers": [
                "License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License",
                "Operating System :: OS Independent",
                "Programming Language :: Python :: 3"
            ],
            "description": "Table of contents\n=================\n\n<!--ts-->\n   * [Out of box colab usage](#out-of-box-colab-usage)\n   * [Getting Started](#getting-started)\n   * [Supported Dataloaders](#dataloaders)\n        * [CIHP](#cihp)\n        * [DIODE](#diode)\n        * [NYUV2](#nyuv2)\n        * [ADE20K](#ade20k)\n        * [Generic Image CSV](#generic)\n        * [NUSCENES](#nuscenes)\n   * [General Development Instructions](#general-development-instructions)\n<!--te-->\n\nOut of box colab usage\n======================\n\n1. Easy monocular depth estimation - https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1T2gONs_gst4zpdS7fBoIaQclgg3J2Jgk?usp=sharing\n\n\nGetting Started\n===============\n\n1. Local system setup \n\n    1. Make sure system is [configured to use nvidia-gpu](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1TscBJu4nX1Q2VVBUJIEgFi6_ijH89-mzsuZ_Whd27V4/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n    2. Make sure [nvidia docker is setup](https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/cloud-native/container-toolkit/install-guide.html)\n\n    OR\n\n2. [Azure cloud computer setup](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1y7JMUwg69t_6jbP-GHrJHk9jQVy3mHcn/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=117914071723364752002&rtpof=true&sd=true)\n\n    After system setup \n\n3. Run the command to get the environment setup (all the relevant libraries) or use the dockerfile here and build env yourself\n    \n    ```bash\n    $ docker pull homagni/mltraining:latest\n    ```\n\n4. git clone this repo\n\n5. Make a folder called \"datasets\" in the same directory where you git cloned this repo\n\n6. Follow the instructions on how to acquire the relevant data from dataloaders instructions below and put the downloaded data in \"datasets\" folder\n\n7. Run the following script to start the docker container by attaching the datasets (example if /home/homag/Desktop/ is the parent directory of the git cloned):\n\n    ```bash\n    $ xhost +\n\n    $ sudo nvidia-docker run --rm -ti --mount type=bind,source=/home/homag/Desktop/ml,target=/ml --mount type=bind,source=/home/homag/Desktop/datasets,target=/datasets --privileged --net=host --ipc=host -e DISPLAY=$DISPLAY -v /tmp/.X11-unix:/tmp/.X11-unix --env=\"QT_X11_NO_MITSHM=1\" homagni/mltraining:latest\n    ```\n\n8. Git ignored file extensions (found in .git/info/exclude)\n    ```bash\n    *.png\n    *.jpg\n    *.jpeg\n    *.pcd\n    *.dat\n    *.pth\n    *.pyc\n    ```\n\n\nSupported Dataloaders\n=====================\nThe dataloaders folder provides high level abstractions to input dataset file names, parse and create a fast accessible numpy memory map and retrieve pytorch compatible batch in a get batch fashion for batchwise training of any network. Each python file in the dataloaders folder can be run as standalone to view a sample of the dataset youre training on. Current supported dataloaders are as follows\n\n CIHP\n ----------------------------------------\nIts a human part segmentation dataset, I modified the loader for entire human pixel segmentation (binary segmentation) link - https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0BzvH3bSnp3E9QjVYZlhWSjltSWM?resourcekey=0-nkS8bDVjPs3bEw3UZW-omA\n\n DIODE\n --------------------------------------\nits a monocular depth estimation dataset, contains RGB and depth maps of indoor and outdoor scenes, download from here - https://diode-dataset.org/ , link - http://diode-dataset.s3.amazonaws.com/train.tar.gz\n \n NYUV2\n -----\nThe famous depth estimation dataset contains scans of indoor scenes for monocular depth estimation, download from here - https://cs.nyu.edu/~silberman/datasets/nyu_depth_v2.html , download individual scenarios using links like these from the website- http://horatio.cs.nyu.edu/mit/silberman/nyu_depth_v2/basements.zip and place each part as a seperate folder in datasets/nyuv2/ for example, datasets/nyuv2/basements/basement_0001a/ will contain all the pgm files\n\n ADE20K\n ------\nThe famous semantic segmentation dataset. The dataloader/ade20k.py has a script that checks folders and downloads the dataset and the object class names automatically the classnames csv is downloaded from [here](https://github.com/CSAILVision/sceneparsing/blob/master/objectInfo150.csv) and then put in /datasets/ADE20K/ folder \n\n Generic Image CSV\n -----------------\nA generic loader for any data annotated as images and corresponding csv annotations for details see dataloaders/imgcsvloader_readme \n\n NUSCENES\n --------\nnuscenes lidar dataset \n\n(steps to acquire)\n\n[login to nuscenes -> go to downloads\n\nnavigate to the nuScenes-lidarseg subsection\n\nselect All-> download Metadata and sensor file blobs by clicking on 'US' mirror link\n\nextract the downloaded folder in nuscenes/nuscenes-lidarseg-all-v1.0\n\nnavigate to Full dataset-> Trainval subsection\n\nclick download Metadata by clicking on 'US' link and save it as nuscenes/v1.0-trainval_meta folder\n\nnow you can download the raw data part by part \n\nfor example navigate to File blobs 85 scenes, part 1, expand the arrow symbol\n\nand can download only the Lidar blobs by again clicking on 'US' link\n\nand can save it as nuscenes/part1/v1.0-trainval01_blobs_lidar\n\ncan do similar thing for camera data as well]\n\n\n\n\n\n\nGeneral Development Instructions\n================================\n\n\nAll projects should be in subdirectories in the applications folder as main.py\nThey can easily call and build upon the submodules - custom_losses, dataloaders, models, trainers, visualizers and applications\n\n\n\ndatacreators:\ncontains several functions that can utilize a bunch of simulators to generate data for various applications\n\n\ncustom_losses:\nProvides high level abstractions to easily define loss functions for specific types of training\n\nmodels:\nDefines a wide variety of model architectures to be used for various scenarios\n\ntrainers:\nDefines an extremely high level abstraction for quick training a model for any general application. Supply the appropriate model, loss function and dataloader classes to it and just call the fit function\n\nvisualizers:\nFor pretty visualization of outputs, defines detailed functions to take care of pointcloud visualization from depth maps as well\n\napplications:\nHere all the modular machine learning solutions are assembled. Just create a specific folder with the name of the project and create a main function that calls all the submodules to load the datasets, define losses and complete the training\n\n\nmisctools:\n\nmiscellaneous tools (code snippets and functions) that can help for various CV related tasks including helping generating data from custom collected data, calibrating cameras/lidars, stiching images, homography, etc. Can also contain starting point code for various applications\n",
            "description_content_type": "text/markdown",
